      ":question/problem": "Find the antiderivative:\n\n$\\displaystyle\\int -\\sqrt[4]{x}-x^{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+C$. Assume $x>0$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int x^p\\,\\mathrm{d}x=x^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int -\\sqrt[4]{x}-x^{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= \\int\\left(-x^{\\frac{1}{4}}-x^{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= -\\int x^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x-\\int x^{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= -\\left(\\frac{x^{\\frac{5}{4}}}{\\frac{5}{4}}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{x^{6}}{6}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -\\frac{4\\sqrt[4]{x^{5}}}{5}-\\frac{x^{6}}{6}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe constant $C$ is already supplied outside the blank.",
